The section 3a arm read `adjudicated = walk_result is True`, so "the walk
exhausted every route and found no dependence" (False) and "the walk lost the
value" (None) were the same event and neither removed anything. False now
REMOVES the flow.

ONE PR FOR TWO ITEMS, and the pairing is load-bearing. Since PR #214 an
unearned False is a live falsehood in a security tool. WI-joluk's forfeit gate
-- downgrade False to None for any function whose CFG statement extents miss a
call node in its body -- is what keeps a False earned. It was already wired to
this arm (2026-08-26); WI-joluk's description still said NOT WIRED, which was
stale. A gate protecting an arm that never consumed a False was a no-op anyone
could have tidied away, so it now has a test.

THREE PROPERTIES KEEP THE REMOVAL EARNED, each independently load-bearing:
  1. the forfeit gate runs first, so an exhausted walk over a demonstrably
     incomplete graph never reaches the removal;
  2. _summary_terminates is a conjunction in which any doubt reads as "no";
  3. the walk is intraprocedural, so "every route" is a claim about one
     function body, not about the program.
`escaped` is IGNORANCE and keeps its flow. `not_attempted` never ran.

THE MEASURED EFFECT IS ZERO AND IS REPORTED AS ZERO. hypergumbo-core:
15 confirmed / 12 escaped / 0 unconfirmed across 27 walks. The 11-repo cohort
reports the same zero (153 ddg_mixed rows: 0 unconfirmed / 14 escaped / 139
not_attempted). These are live semantics over a currently EMPTY class; they
activate as escape sites close, and the survivors on the self-tree belong to
INV-mumov (1) and INV-linub (3). Forcing every escape closed yields 7 False on
that corpus -- a CEILING, not a prediction, since that arm also closes callees
that genuinely propagate. The filing's pre-registered "~13% fewer evidence
rows" cannot be scored against a corpus that produces no refutations, so it is
re-registered against the escape-closure work rather than claimed as met.

A REMOVAL IS NEVER SILENT. verify-claims publishes
dataflow_coverage.flows_removed_by_walk on both the JSON and text surfaces,
counted separately from findings_total (which counts survivors). A security
tool whose findings can be fewer than its analysis produced, with no number a
reader can check, is the failure mode this prevents. The first live run printed
0.

THE CHANGE FALSIFIED A PUBLISHED CLAIM IN TEN PLACES AND THE R16 TRIGGER BUILT
FOR EXACTLY THIS CAUGHT IT. dataflow_coverage.inclusion_decided_by was the
constant "call_graph_reachability", justified by section 3a being confirm-only,
with a test whose docstring said it must change when section 3a gained
refutation. It fired. The value is now
"call_graph_reachability_minus_ddg_refutation" -- compound because BOTH halves
stay true: the walk mints nothing, so inclusion is still bounded by
reachability, and reading analysis_method == "ddg" as "inclusion decided by
data flow" is still the INV-sadah misreading. Also corrected: the rendered text
disclosure ("never removes a flow"), TaintFlowFinding.walk_verdict's docstring
("RECORDED, NOT ACTED ON" -- it is now the field that decides removal), six
further taint.py comments, the ADR section 3a banner, and two spec paragraphs.

WHAT IS BEING TRADED, IN WRITING. ADR-0017 section 7b excludes alias analysis
and states a preference for overapproximation, so this introduces FALSE
NEGATIVES on container and alias mutation. The owner granted that trade
explicitly on 2026-09-02; it is not one the implementation may make on its own.

